$$\begin{matrix}
      A_{11} & {= \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      3 & 0 & {- 1} \\
      {- 5} & 2 & 4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,} & {\quad A_{12}} & {= \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      5 & 9 \\
      0 & {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,} & {\quad A_{13}} & {= \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      A_{21} & {= \begin{bmatrix}
      {- 8} & {- 6} & 3
      \end{bmatrix},} & {\quad A_{22}} & {= \begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 7
      \end{bmatrix},} & {\quad A_{23}} & {= \begin{bmatrix}
      {- 4}
      \end{bmatrix}}
      \end{matrix}$$

$$\begin{array}{lll}
      {\text{col}_{1}(A)\text{~row}_{1}(B)} & = & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {- 3} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      a & b
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      {- 3a} & {- 3b} \\
      a & b
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {\text{col}_{2}(A)\text{~row}_{2}(B)} & = & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      1 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      c & d
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      c & d \\
      {- 4c} & {- 4d}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {\text{col}_{3}(A)\text{~row}_{3}(B)} & = & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      2 \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      e & f
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      {2e} & {2f} \\
      {5e} & {5f}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$

$$\begin{bmatrix}
      A_{11} & A_{12} \\
      0 & A_{22}
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      B_{11} & B_{12} \\
      B_{21} & B_{22}
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      I_{p} & 0 \\
      0 & I_{p}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

$\begin{bmatrix}
      A & B \\
      C & 0
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      I & 0 \\
      X & Y
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      0 & I \\
      Z & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$

$\begin{bmatrix}
      X & 0 \\
      Y & Z
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      A & 0 \\
      B & C
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      I & 0 \\
      0 & I
      \end{bmatrix}$

$\begin{bmatrix}
      A & B \\
      0 & I
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      X & Y & Z \\
      0 & 0 & I
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      I & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & I
      \end{bmatrix}$

statement.paragraph:
Suppose $A_{11}$ is an invertible matrix. Find matrices $X$ and $Y$ such that the product below has the form indicated. Also, compute $B_{22}$. [Hint:Compute the product on the left, and set it equal to the right side.] $$\begin{bmatrix}
      I & 0 & 0 \\
      X & I & 0 \\
      Y & 0 & I
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      A_{11} & A_{12} \\
      A_{21} & A_{22} \\
      A_{31} & A_{32}
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      B_{11} & B_{12} \\
      0 & B_{22} \\
      0 & B_{32}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

(T/F) If $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      A_{11} & A_{12} \\
      A_{21} & A_{22}
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \begin{bmatrix}
      B_{1} \\
      B_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$, then the partitions of $A$ and $B$ are conformable for block multiplication.

(T/F) If $A_{1}$, $A_{2}$, $B_{1}$, and $B_{2}$ are $n \times n$ matrices, $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      A_{1} \\
      A_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$, and $B = \begin{bmatrix}
      B_{1} & B_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$, then the product $BA$ is defined, but $AB$ is not.

Suppose the transfer function $W(s)$ in Exercise 21 is invertible for some $s$. It can be shown that the inverse transfer function $W(s)^{- 1}$, which transforms outputs into inputs, is the Schur complement of $A - BC - sI_{n}$ for the matrix below. Find this Schur complement. See Exercise 17.$$\begin{bmatrix}
      {A - BC - sI_{n}} & B \\
      {- C} & I_{m}
      \end{bmatrix}$$
